<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # There Are So Many Types of Clubs! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<br>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> There Are So Many Types of Clubs!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects. Reading: Derive the meaning of simple unknown words from short texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can distinguish between facts and opinions in announcements, posters, or newsletters about school events (e.g., Fact: "The music club practices every Thursday." Opinion: "The music club is the most fun because it's energetic.").
Can find the meaning of unknown words in a text about club activities (e.g., "I think in this sentence, 'engage' means to participate actively.").
Can separate factual and opinion-based statements in a list of club benefits (e.g., Fact: "It is beneficial to join social clubs." Opinion: "I believe joining a club improves your social skills.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various school clubs (e.g., a sports team, a science fair project, a debate club, an art group). Students briefly discuss in pairs what they see. The teacher asks, "What types of clubs do you know from your school or community?" and "What interests do these clubs represent?" This activates prior vocabulary like <i>clubs, interests, sports, activities, team, project, community</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher reads aloud a short description of a fictional school club, for example, "The 'Green Team' is an environmental club. Members *plan* and *organize* activities to improve our school garden. Last year, they *engaged* in a big clean-up *project*." The teacher models the pronunciation of /pr/ in "project" and /tr/ in "team." The teacher also emphasizes the stress on the imperative "Let's plan activities."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks comprehension and clarifies key vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the 'Green Team' a sports club or an environmental club?" (Checking basic understanding)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I said, 'They *engaged* in a big clean-up project,' what do you think 'engaged' means?" (Prompting inference of unknown words)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the club just talk about problems, or do they *plan* and *organize* real *activities*?" (Reinforcing verbs)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides a handout with a short article from a fictional "School Clubs Newsletter" or a set of club announcements/posters. This text describes several school clubs, including their *goals*, *activities*, *members*, *requirements*, and benefits. The text includes both factual statements (e.g., "The Science Club meets every Tuesday in Lab B, *across from* the library.") and opinion-based statements (e.g., "It's the most exciting club for curious minds!"). The text utilizes target vocabulary such as <i>structured, organized, collaborative, participate, manage, coordinate, social, responsibilities, leadership</i>. It also provides context for deriving meaning of potentially unknown words like "facilitate" or "delegate."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading for specific information and differentiating between objective facts and subjective opinions within club descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of this newsletter section?" "Which clubs are mentioned in the article?" (Focus on global comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students answer True/False questions based on the text. Example: "The Chess Club practices on Fridays." (True/False - Factual) "The Drama Club is definitely the most entertaining club." (True/False - Opinion-based, leading to the next stage).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Matching:</b> Match definitions or synonyms to selected words from the text, focusing on words like 'engage', 'coordinate', 'delegate', 'facilitate'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Distinguishing facts from opinions; deriving meaning of unknown words. Grammar: Simple present for descriptions, simple past for club history, imperatives for instructions. Vocabulary: <i>clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, requirements, responsibilities, engage, participate, social, structured, organized, collaborative, manage, coordinate, feedback, suggestions</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Fact vs. Opinion Sort (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a list of 8-10 statements extracted from the newsletter article. Students work individually to classify each statement as either a "Fact" or an "Opinion" in a two-column table. Example: "The Photography Club meets twice a month." (Fact) vs. "Photography is the most rewarding hobby." (Opinion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Guess the Meaning (Semi-controlled):</b> Students revisit the article and highlight 3-4 words they found difficult (e.g., 'facilitate', 'delegate', 'inclusive', 'survey'). In pairs, they discuss the context of each word and try to infer its meaning, writing a short definition or a synonym. The teacher circulates, offering minimal support and encouraging strategies like looking at surrounding words. Example: "I think 'engage' in this sentence means to participate actively."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Club Profile Analysis (Pair Interaction):</b> Students in pairs choose two clubs from the article. They re-read the descriptions and underline all the facts in one color and opinions in another. They then write down one imperative from each club's description (e.g., "Set goals," "Let's plan activities") and one simple past sentence about a past event of the club (e.g., "Last year, the Chess Club *arranged* a tournament."). They then compare their findings with another pair, clarifying any disagreements.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Discussion and Recommendation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students imagine they are a committee deciding which two existing clubs to promote heavily for new *members*. They are given two new, slightly longer club descriptions (e.g., "The Digital Arts Club" and "The Community Service Network"). These descriptions are richer in both factual details and persuasive opinions, and contain a mix of target vocabulary (e.g., *collaborative, responsible, contribute, leadership, negotiate roles, arrange meetings*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives two new club descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First, each student reads both descriptions silently, identifying 3-4 facts and 3-4 opinions for each club, and noting down any unknown words and their inferred meanings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, in their groups, students discuss the clubs. They must clearly distinguish between facts ("The Digital Arts Club *manages* a school blog") and opinions ("This club offers the most creative outlet for students"). They also discuss any words they inferred meaning for.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they *negotiate* and decide which one club they would recommend for promotion, justifying their choice by explicitly using facts and opinions from the texts. They use simple present to describe the clubs and simple past to refer to any past activities mentioned. For instance, "I like this club because we can *engage* in many *activities* together. Last year, they *organized* a successful *event*." or "We need to *set goals* for promotion."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group prepares a 1-minute oral recommendation for the class, explaining their chosen club and why they believe it's the best to promote, emphasizing the distinction between facts and opinions in their reasoning. They also share one new word they learned to infer.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and oral recommendations, focusing on reading comprehension skills (fact/opinion distinction, meaning derivation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> As an exit ticket, each student writes a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing their group's chosen club for promotion. In this paragraph, they must include at least two facts and two opinions from the club description and use one of the new vocabulary words (e.g., *coordinate*, *delegate*, *facilitate*, *engage*) with its inferred meaning. They should clearly label which statements are facts and which are opinions (e.g., "Fact: ... Opinion: ...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in distinguishing between facts and opinions (main objective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in deriving meaning of unknown words from context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (<i>members, activities, goals, responsibilities, engage, coordinate, participate, social, structured, organized</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and comprehensibility of the written summary.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1-5, how confident am I in distinguishing between facts and opinions in a text about clubs?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was I able to find the meaning of unknown words using the context?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has distinguishing facts from opinions improved my understanding of club descriptions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did I use today to understand new words in the text?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully *distinguish* between facts and opinions? What were the common challenges?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the chosen texts and vocabulary appropriate for B1.1 students? Were they able to *derive* meaning effectively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students struggling with inferring meaning or identifying subjective language?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we read and understood different club descriptions. In our next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), we will use this information to *discuss* and *negotiate* our roles and *responsibilities* when planning our own school club. Be ready to ask about club preferences!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>